=> We will build an Apache Web Server Role.

What we are going to build

Our project will look like this:

ansible-roles-lab/
├── inventory
├── site.yml
└── roles/
    └── apache/
        ├── tasks/
        │   └── main.yml
        ├── handlers/
        │   └── main.yml
        ├── templates/
        │   └── index.html.j2
        ├── files/
        ├── defaults/
        │   └── main.yml
        ├── vars/
        │   └── main.yml
        ├── meta/
        │   └── main.yml
        └── README.md

=> In the lab, we'll mainly use:

tasks/
handlers/
templates/
defaults/

These are the most useful ones for understanding how roles work.

1. Create the project

In your WSL:

mkdir ansible-roles-lab
cd ansible-roles-lab

Create the inventory:

nano inventory

Example:

[web]
server1 ansible_host=YOUR_SERVER_IP

[web:vars]
ansible_user=ubuntu

Replace:

YOUR_SERVER_IP

with your EC2/Ubuntu server IP.

Because you already understand passwordless authentication, Ansible should be able to connect using your SSH key.

Test it:

ansible all -i inventory -m ping

Expected:

server1 | SUCCESS => {
    "ping": "pong"
}

2. Create the Role

Now comes the important part.

Run:

ansible-galaxy init roles/apache

This command automatically creates the standard role structure.

Check it:

tree roles/apache

You should see something like this

roles/apache
├── README.md
├── defaults
│   └── main.yml
├── files
├── handlers
│   └── main.yml
├── meta
│   └── main.yml
├── tasks
│   └── main.yml
├── templates
├── tests
│   ├── inventory
│   └── test.yml
└── vars
    └── main.yml

Instead of manually creating all these directories, Ansible gives you the standard structure.

3. Understand the role before writing anything

Think of our Apache role like this:

apache role
     |
     +-- tasks       → Install/configure Apache
     |
     +-- handlers    → Restart Apache when required
     |
     +-- templates   → Dynamic configuration/page
     |
     +-- defaults    → Default values
     |
     +-- vars        → Role variables

4. Create the Default Variables

Open:

nano roles/apache/defaults/main.yml

Put:

apache_package: apache2
apache_service: apache2
website_title: "My Ansible Web Server"

These are our default values.

For example:

apache_package: apache2
apache_service: apache2
website_title: "My Ansible Web Server"

These are our default values.

For example:

apache_package: apache2

means:

By default, install the apache2 package.

And:

website_title: "My Ansible Web Server"

means:

If nobody provides another title, use "My Ansible Web Server".

5. Create the Template

Now create:

nano roles/apache/templates/index.html.j2

Put:

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>{{ website_title }}</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>{{ website_title }}</h1>
    <p>This server was configured using Ansible Roles.</p>
</body>
</html>

Notice:

{{ website_title }}

This is the important part.

It is a variable.

The value comes from:

defaults/main.yml

So:

defaults/main.yml
        |
        | website_title
        ↓
index.html.j2
        |
        | {{ website_title }}
        ↓
Final index.html

The final server page will contain:

<h1>My Ansible Web Server</h1>

6. Create the Tasks

Now open:

nano roles/apache/tasks/main.yml

Put:

- name: Install Apache
  ansible.builtin.apt:
    name: "{{ apache_package }}"
    state: present
    update_cache: true

- name: Start Apache
  ansible.builtin.service:
    name: "{{ apache_service }}"
    state: started
    enabled: true

- name: Deploy website
  ansible.builtin.template:
    src: index.html.j2
    dest: /var/www/html/index.html
    mode: '0644'
  notify: Restart Apache

=> Let's understand this carefully.

a) Task 1
- name: Install Apache

Ansible installs Apache.

But notice:

name: "{{ apache_package }}"

We are not writing:

name: apache2

Instead, we're using our variable:

apache_package

whose default value is:

apache2

b) Task 2
- name: Start Apache

It starts Apache and enables it at boot.

Again:

name: "{{ apache_service }}"

The value comes from:

apache_service: apache2

c) Task 3

This is the interesting one:

- name: Deploy website
  ansible.builtin.template:

The template module takes:

templates/index.html.j2

and creates:

/var/www/html/index.html

on the remote server.

7. Create the Handler

Now open:

nano roles/apache/handlers/main.yml

Put:

- name: Restart Apache
  ansible.builtin.service:
    name: apache2
    state: restarted

Now look back at our task:

notify: Restart Apache

This means:

If the website template changes, notify the Restart Apache handler.

So the flow is:

Template changed
       ↓
Task reports "changed"
       ↓
notify: Restart Apache
       ↓
Handler runs
       ↓
Apache restarts

8. Create the Main Playbook

Now create:

nano site.yml

Put:

- name: Configure Apache Web Server
  hosts: web
  become: true

  roles:
    - apache

This is now a very clean playbook.

The playbook doesn't contain all the Apache installation tasks.

It simply says:

roles:
  - apache


