10. Run the Role

Run:

ansible-playbook -i inventory site.yml

You should see tasks similar to:

TASK [apache : Install Apache]
TASK [apache : Start Apache]
TASK [apache : Deploy website]
RUNNING HANDLER [apache : Restart Apache]

Notice this:

apache : Install Apache

The apache part tells you that the task is coming from the apache role.

11. curl http://localhost

to check whether the web server (Apache) is actually working and serving the webpage.

=> Break it down

a);curl is a command-line tool used to make requests to a URL.

curl http://localhost

means:

"Send an HTTP request to the web server running on this same machine and show me its response."

Here:

curl → makes the HTTP request
http:// → use HTTP protocol
localhost → this same server (127.0.0.1)

You should see:

<h1>My Ansible Web Server</h1>

You can also open:

http://YOUR_SERVER_IP

in your browser if port 80 is allowed in the AWS Security Group.

12. Now Understand the Real Power of defaults

This is where the lab becomes more useful.

Currently:

website_title: "My Ansible Web Server"

is in:

roles/apache/defaults/main.yml

Suppose you want another title.

You don't have to modify the role.

You can override the value from the playbook:

- name: Configure Apache Web Server
  hosts: web
  become: true

  vars:
    website_title: "Production Web Server"

  roles:
    - apache

Now the template:

{{ website_title }}

will use:

Production Web Server

instead of:

My Ansible Web Server

This is a very important concept in real projects.


13. What happens when you run the playbook?

The complete flow is:

ansible-playbook site.yml
          ↓
      Playbook
          ↓
    roles: apache
          ↓
     Apache Role
          ↓
     tasks/main.yml
          ↓
 ┌────────┼───────────┐
 ↓        ↓           ↓
Install  Start     Template
Apache   Apache     Website
                     ↓
              defaults/main.yml
                     ↓
              website_title
                     ↓
             Final index.html

If the template changes:

Template changed
      ↓
notify
      ↓
Handler
      ↓
Restart Apache

14. How this looks in an industry project

In a real project, you might have:

roles/
├── apache/
├── nginx/
├── docker/
├── users/
├── monitoring/
├── security/
├── java/
└── application/

Then the main playbook might be:

- name: Configure Production Servers
  hosts: web
  become: true

  roles:
    - users
    - security
    - docker
    - monitoring
    - application

Each role has its own responsibility.

For example:

users role
   ↓
Create required users

security role
   ↓
Configure security settings

docker role
   ↓
Install Docker

monitoring role
   ↓
Install monitoring agent

application role
   ↓
Deploy application


This is much easier to maintain than putting everything into one 500–1000 line playbook.


Note:-

For example:

              Ansible Project
                     |
       ┌─────────────┼─────────────┐
       ↓             ↓             ↓
   Web Role      Docker Role   Monitoring Role
       |             |             |
    Apache        Docker        Agent
       |             |             |
   templates      tasks        handlers
   handlers       vars         templates
   defaults       defaults

That's the main reason companies use Ansible Roles: each responsibility is separated, reusable, and easier for the team to maintain.





